[stderr] -### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations
[stderr] +### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy
[stderr]  
[stderr] -**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages → validates → activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent → acquired → changed/equipped/used → depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty → writing → confirmed → loading → confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed → deleting → deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged → cached → active → superseded → eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored → reviewed/licensed-original → packaged → referenced → retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft → reviewed → validated → packaged → released → retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open → fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built → validated → approved → published → supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created → minimised/scrubbed → queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected → triaged → mitigated → resolved; `TestCase` is authored → run → passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.
[stderr] +
[stderr] +**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.
