town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n179:| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n180:| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n181:| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n182:| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n183:| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n184:| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n185:| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n186:| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n187:| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n188:| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n189:| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n190:| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n191:| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n192:| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n193:| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n194:| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n195:| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n197:## Cross-target contract\n208:## First-release completeness proof\n210:J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n212:## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..9ee258c6bf8f61b377cf6fa3c34da72ee6fbe3a2\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,215 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n+\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+## Core journeys\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n+\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n+\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short; git diff --check --no-index /dev/null projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md' in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n exited 3 in 0ms:\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-2/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-3/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest/\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:215: new blank line at EOF.\n\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..9ee258c6bf8f61b377cf6fa3c34da72ee6fbe3a2\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,215 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n+\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+## Core journeys\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n+\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n+\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n\napply patch\npatch: completed\n/Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,214 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+## Core journeys\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n+\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n+\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n+\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n\nhook: PostToolUse\nhook: PostToolUse Failed\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,214 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+## Core journeys\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n+\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n+\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n+\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff --check --no-index /dev/null projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md; test -s projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md; rg -c '\"'^### [0-9]+'\"\\\\.' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md; rg -c '| P0 \u2014 first release |' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n12\n214\n\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,214 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+## Core journeys\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n+\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n+\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n+\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n\ncodex\n{\"status\":\"pass\",\"summary\":\"Created the feature model for the offline 2D fantasy RPG, including journeys, domain chapters, lifecycle rules, relationship invariants, feature slices, and web/PWA parity.\",\"changed_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\"],\"test_result\":\"Passed: `git diff --check --no-index /dev/null projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md`; confirmed the specification is non-empty; confirmed 12 domain chapters are present.\",\"evidence\":{\"agent_log\":\"Reviewed the discovery map and generated a domain-specific feature specification that preserves unresolved connected-service decisions instead of inventing policy.\",\"adapter_log\":\"\",\"test_log\":\"Whitespace check passed after removing the trailing blank line at EOF.\",\"browser_log\":\"Not applicable: this task changes domain documentation only.\",\"screenshots\":[]},\"acceptance_results\":[{\"criterion\":\"Every feature maps to a user outcome and journey.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The prioritised feature catalogue maps every listed feature to outcomes and J1\u2013J5 journeys.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Normal, empty, invalid, permission-denied, offline, conflict, recovery, and deletion paths are specified where relevant.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"Core journeys and each applicable domain chapter explicitly describe normal and relevant exceptional paths, including storage denial, offline operation, future remote-conflict boundary, recovery, and deletion.\"},{\"criterion\":\"The first release provides one complete useful journey without deferred dependencies.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The First-release completeness proof establishes J1 from anonymous launch through first battle and confirmed local save, with no account, multiplayer, commerce, or live-service dependency.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Cross-target capability parity and intentional target-specific differences are documented.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The Cross-target contract documents shared campaign/input/save behavior and intentional PWA install/cache/update differences.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Each domain chapter defines its entities, rules, lifecycle, authoring inputs, dependencies, player/user outcomes, edge cases, and first-release boundary.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"Twelve domain chapters cover shell, gameplay, content, platform, quality, and operations; each includes entities/lifecycle, rules/inputs/dependencies/outcome, edges, and release boundary.\"},{\"criterion\":\"The relationship map identifies cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries. For example, an RPG quest links narrative and save state; an IDE edit links language analysis, workspace indexing, and version control; an OS permission links identity, process, storage, and user settings.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The system relationship map and six cross-domain invariants explicitly link narrative, quests, combat rewards, inventory, content packs, persistence, recovery, and PWA updates.\"}],\"documentation\":{\"reviewed_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/00-domain-discovery.md\",\"projects/README.md\"],\"updated_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\"],\"summary\":\"Added the canonical F002 feature-model specification. No README change was needed because this documents a project-specific domain model and does not alter repository usage.\"},\"known_limitations\":[\"Engine/framework, browser/device/performance thresholds, content quantities, localisation scope, accessibility conformance target, remote diagnostics policy, connected services, commerce, and legal/store policy remain approval decisions recorded in the specification.\"],\"browser_coverage\":\"Not applicable: domain documentation only; no browser-facing implementation or Playwright specification changed.\",\"recommended_next_step\":\"complete\"}\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 020702b2a410cbe74dcade94a047bd014bf63347..0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,144 +1,214 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md), [normalized brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md), and [source brief](../INITIAL.md). This model preserves unresolved decisions rather than selecting services or technology.\n+**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n \n-## Product, personas, and outcomes\n+## Product boundary\n+\n+Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n \n-The first release is an original, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG: explore one town and one dungeon, advance a short quest chain through turn-based combat and rewards, save/resume, and reach a short ending. Responsive web and installable PWA deliver the same shared game core; neither has exclusive story or rules.\n+The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n+\n+## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n \n-| Persona | Job to be done | First useful outcome | Needs |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual campaign player | Make visible story progress in a short session without a complex system. | Finish the tutorial battle, save, and know how to return. | Clear goals, readable choices, forgiving recovery, no required connection. |\n-| Returning player | Resume after interruption or time away without losing confirmed progress. | Load/resume and see the current quest context. | Checkpoints, save status, safe updates and storage recovery. |\n-| Access-needs player | Play the same campaign with preferred input and reading settings. | Complete onboarding with usable controls and understandable feedback. | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; text, captions, visual settings, responsive UI. |\n+| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n+| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n+| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n+| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n \n-| Journey | Happy path and outcome | Relevant alternate paths |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1 First session | Start \u2192 onboarding \u2192 town objective \u2192 tutorial encounter \u2192 win/reward \u2192 confirmed save. Player understands combat and saving. | Empty save list starts new game; invalid/missing content produces recovery UI; input changes retain actionable controls; cached game plays offline. |\n-| J2 Campaign | Load/new \u2192 town dialogue/quest \u2192 dungeon/encounters \u2192 rewards/inventory \u2192 quest completion \u2192 ending. Player completes the compact story. | Locked objective names prerequisite; empty/invalid inventory is explained; defeat returns to checkpoint; invalid state restores last confirmed save. |\n-| J3 Resume/recovery | Open \u2192 select confirmed save \u2192 resume \u2192 pause/save \u2192 return after interruption or update. Player loses no confirmed progress. | No save offers new game; corrupt/incompatible save is quarantined with choices; storage permission/quota denial keeps prior save and explains recovery; asset cache never overwrites save data. |\n-| J4 Target delivery | Open web or install PWA \u2192 set input/settings \u2192 play J1/J2. Same campaign works on supported surface. | Unsupported gamepad falls back to advertised input; declined install leaves web play; uncached offline asset asks for reconnection without deleting saves. |\n+Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n \n-Every P0 feature maps to J1\u2013J4 below; no P0 feature depends on unresolved connected scope.\n+## Core journeys\n \n-## Vocabulary, entities, and lifecycles\n+| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n+| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n+| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n+| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n+| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+\n+## Domain vocabulary\n \n-| Term/entity | Meaning, lifecycle, and rules |\n+| Term | Meaning and rule |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | Authored town-to-dungeon-to-ending progression. Definitions are content-owned; player completion is progression-owned. |\n-| Player profile | Local preferences and save index. An account is not assumed. |\n-| Save slot/snapshot | Named, versioned confirmed serialization: absent \u2192 new \u2192 checkpointed/confirmed \u2192 loaded \u2192 superseded or deletion-requested \u2192 deleted. Validate before session creation; failed migration/corruption leaves prior artifact untouched. Delete requires confirmation and affects only the selected slot. |\n-| Session | Runtime state derived from a valid snapshot; never the only durable progress record. Pause does not imply save. |\n-| Scene/trigger | Authored town/dungeon, collision, interactive object, exit, or encounter trigger: unavailable/idle \u2192 eligible \u2192 active \u2192 resolved/disabled. Broken destination/content is recoverable, never a soft-lock. |\n-| Quest/objective | Authored objective graph: unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 completed or failed \u2192 reward granted. Prerequisites and completion/reward are explicit and idempotent. |\n-| Encounter/combatant | Bounded turn-based event: armed \u2192 initiated \u2192 legal turn cycle \u2192 resolved (victory/defeat/authorised escape) \u2192 rewards/checkpoint eligible. Invalid actor/action/target changes no state or turn. |\n-| Item/inventory | Definition and stack: defined \u2192 granted \u2192 held \u2192 used/equipped/consumed or removed. Quantity is never negative; eligibility is validated. Any capacity policy must retain recoverable rewards. |\n-| Content package | Original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, art/audio and locale keys: drafted \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 cached/active \u2192 retired. Partial/incompatible package retains last known-good content. |\n-| Diagnostic record | detected \u2192 minimised/consent-gated \u2192 queued/sent \u2192 expired/deleted. It never blocks play or save; provider, retention, consent and deletion are open decisions. |\n+| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n+| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n+| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n+| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n+| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n+| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n+| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n+| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n+| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n+| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n+| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n+| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n \n-## System relationship map\n+## System relationship map and invariants\n \n ```text\n-Authoring/validation \u2500packages\u2500> Content (scenes, dialogue, quests, items,\n-                                encounters, localisation, art/audio)\n-                                      \u2502                    \u2502\n-Input + rendering + accessibility \u2500> Exploration \u2500interacts\u2500> Narrative/quests\n-        \u2502                              \u2502                         \u2502\n-        \u2502                              \u2514\u2500starts\u2500> Combat \u2500> Rewards/inventory\n-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500settings apply to all player UI\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                                                            \u2502\n-                          Progression <\u2500\u2500completion/reward\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-                               \u2502\n-Shell/session <\u2500\u2500loads\u2500\u2500 Save/checkpoint <\u2500\u2500serialises\u2500\u2500 shared game state\n-     \u2502                         \u2502                                   \u2502\n-     \u251c\u2500PWA cache/update\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500must never mutate\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n-     \u2514\u2500error boundary/diagnostics \u2500\u2500> player recovery/support triage\n-\n-Release and quality validate every edge. Privacy/security constrain diagnostics\n-and any approved connected boundary. Account/sync, multiplayer, live operations,\n-and commerce remain optional interfaces outside the offline loop.\n+Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n+                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n+Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n+Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n+All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n+PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n+Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n+Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n+accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n ```\n \n-**Invariants:** a quest completion, reward grant, and progression flag persist as one transaction; an encounter changes party resources, inventory, quest state, and checkpoint eligibility only at its defined resolution boundary; save content identifiers validate against compatible content before resume; settings affect presentation/input rather than rules; and asset-cache activation can never delete, replace, or reinterpret a confirmed save.\n+Cross-domain invariants:\n \n+1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n+2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n+3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n+4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n+5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n+6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n+\n+Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n+\n ## Domain chapters\n \n-### Game shell, session, and navigation\n+### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n+\n+### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n+\n+### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n+\n+### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** boot, title/menu, session, pause, recovery modal, and scene transition. Boot validates active content and save index; selecting a valid snapshot creates a session; quit ends runtime only. **Rules/outcomes:** new game, load/resume, settings, pause, permitted save, and return-to-menu make J1/J3 safe. No-save is normal; invalid slot returns recovery UI; reload/crash returns through validation. **Inputs/dependencies:** persistence, content version, rendering/input, recovery UI. **P0 boundary:** excludes social/home feed and login.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n \n-### Exploration and scenes\n+### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** scene definitions, location, collision, interactions, exits, triggers. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J2 traverse exactly one town and one dungeon, find objectives, and transition to dialogue/combat. Collision applies; locked routes explain prerequisites; empty/broken scene content triggers recovery rather than soft-lock. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored maps/collision/exits and validation; input/rendering, quests, combat, saves. **P0 boundary:** no extra, procedural, or user-created regions.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n \n-### Narrative, dialogue, and ending\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** character, dialogue node/choice, locale key, narrative flag, ending: eligible \u2192 presented \u2192 choice/advance \u2192 recorded outcome. **Rules/outcomes:** original readable dialogue explains J2 and a short ending concludes it. Empty optional dialogue has neutral fallback; missing required text/localisation blocks delivery; effects are explicit and persisted. **Inputs/dependencies:** original writing, localisation, feedback, quest flags, saves. **P0 boundary:** compact campaign only; no copied reference material or expansion.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n \n-### Quests, progression, onboarding, and rewards\n+### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** quest graph, objective, prerequisite, tutorial step, flag, reward grant; onboarding unstarted \u2192 prompted \u2192 completed/resumed. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 teaches first combat and saving; J2 exposes an actionable objective. Prerequisites block invalid skips; repeat completion cannot duplicate reward; invalid data restores prior confirmed state. **Inputs/dependencies:** dialogue, exploration, combat, inventory, validation, saves. **P0 boundary:** only ending path, not daily/repeatable/event quests.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n \n-### Party combat and balance\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** party member, enemy, ability, target, resource/status, encounter, result. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 tutorialises first battle; J2 gives clear turn feedback. Validate actor/action/target/resource/turn before mutation. Empty ability/no legal target/invalid command has controlled messaging; defeat follows checkpoint recovery. **Inputs/dependencies:** authored encounters/abilities/enemies; scenes, feedback, progression, rewards, saves. **P0 boundary:** no multiplayer/competitive features. Party size, formulae, count and balance targets require approval.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n \n-### Inventory and equipment\n+### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** item definition, stack, inventory, equipment slot, reward claim. **Rules/outcomes:** J2 supports readable inspection and legal use/equip. Empty inventory is instructional; invalid item/target/quantity changes nothing. **Inputs/dependencies:** item content, combat/quest rewards, UI/accessibility, saves. **P0 boundary:** no trade, crafting, market, or paid items. Capacity is undecided: avoid it or define unclaimed-reward recovery before adding it.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n \n-### Persistence, offline recovery, and data lifecycle\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** index, manual save, checkpoint, schema, backup/candidate, deletion. **Rules/outcomes:** J1 demonstrates save; J3 covers resume, corruption, storage denial/quota, incompatible data, no-data, migration and deletion. Preserve prior confirmed state on failed write/migration/update and show recovery; selected-slot deletion is confirmed. Remote sync conflict requires explicit future policy, never silent choice. **Inputs/dependencies:** storage and content version; supports shell, combat, inventory, progression, PWA. **P0 boundary:** local offline-capable persistence; no account/sync.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n \n-### Presentation, input, accessibility, and localisation\n+### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** responsive layout, action/binding, focus, settings profile, caption cue, visual setting, locale bundle. Settings load \u2192 edit \u2192 apply \u2192 persist. **Rules/outcomes:** J1/J4 work with keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where browser/device support permits; dialogue/inventory/quest/progression UI stays readable on phone/tablet/desktop. Captions accompany relevant feedback. Permission-denied/unsupported hardware must not trap play; provide alternate advertised input. **Inputs/dependencies:** every UI domain, original assets, locale content, platform APIs. **P0 boundary:** no target-exclusive rules; language set/exact settings are open.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+\n+### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+\n+### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+\n+### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n \n-### Original content, assets, authoring, and delivery\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** source assets and gameplay definitions, validation report, versioned package: draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active/retired. **Rules/outcomes:** delivers original warm hand-painted, readable content for the complete P0 campaign. Validation checks required strings, links, exits, encounters, IDs, and asset availability; invalid packages cannot activate. **Inputs/dependencies:** feeds gameplay/presentation and release. **P0 boundary:** no copied assets/content, UGC/mod loading, or seasonal cadence.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n \n-### PWA/platform, release, quality, and operations\n+### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** install status, service-worker/cache version, candidate, active/rollback package, test evidence, error boundary, diagnostic event. Cache stages \u2192 validates \u2192 activates; rollback restores known-good assets, never saves. **Rules/outcomes:** J4 is installable and offline after caching. Offline asset miss, interrupted refresh, update, storage denial, and browser-feature denial are visible/recoverable. QA covers gameplay, persistence/recovery, offline/update, responsive/input/accessibility, compatibility, and approved performance budgets. Crash UI offers restart/menu/recovery where safe. **Inputs/dependencies:** package/version contracts, persistence, shell, privacy policy. **P0 boundary:** no backend uptime, paid support, or live events; support matrix/budgets/provider/rollback process need approval.\n+**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n \n-### Privacy, security, identity, connected services, and commerce boundary\n+**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n \n-**Entities/lifecycle:** consent, diagnostic record, account, remote replica, report/moderation case, entitlement. Only minimal diagnostics are a required candidate; all others are dormant interfaces. **Rules/outcomes:** collection/transmission requires approved minimisation, consent, retention, access and deletion policy. Permission-denied/offline connected paths preserve local play; future conflict policy is explicit. Purchases need entitlement/refund/regional/outage behavior; multiplayer needs age/safety/reporting/moderation/operations ownership. **Inputs/dependencies:** approved vendor, legal, security, operations. **P0 boundary:** account/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, purchases/entitlements, advanced analytics and monetisation do not ship until their contradiction is resolved.\n+**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n \n ## Prioritised feature catalogue\n \n-| Priority/feature | Outcome/journey | Slice | Dependencies |\n+| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 F01 Shell/new/resume | Safely begin or return (J1/J3). | New, load, pause, recovery navigation. | Saves, content, rendering. |\n-| P0 F02 Exploration | Progress campaign (J1/J2). | One town, one dungeon, interactions. | Content, input, quests, combat. |\n-| P0 F03 Narrative/ending | Understand and finish story (J2). | Original dialogue and short ending. | Content, locale, progression. |\n-| P0 F04 Quests/onboarding | Learn and know next goal (J1/J2). | Tutorial, bounded quest chain/rewards. | Narrative, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F05 Turn combat | Complete encounters (J1/J2). | Authored combat and defeat recovery. | Content, progression, inventory. |\n-| P0 F06 Inventory/rewards | Understand/use rewards (J2). | Inspect, grant, legal use/equip. | Content, combat, saves. |\n-| P0 F07 Persistence | Resume without silent loss (J1/J3). | Local saves, checkpoints, validation, recovery, deletion. | Storage, content version. |\n-| P0 F08 Accessible responsive input/settings | Play/read with preferences (J1/J4). | Responsive UI, inputs, text/captions/visual settings. | UI domains, platform APIs. |\n-| P0 F09 Original content delivery | Coherent original campaign (J1/J2). | Validated original packages/assets. | Authoring, release. |\n-| P0 F10 PWA/offline/update | Install and continue safely (J3/J4). | Installability, offline assets/saves, safe cache update. | Persistence, release. |\n-| P0 F11 Error recovery/quality | Recover from failure (J3/J4). | Player recovery UI, approved minimal diagnostics, target testing. | Shell, privacy decisions. |\n-| Gate G01 Account/remote state | Optional continuity only if approved. | Not P0; local profile/save only. | Privacy/security, backend. |\n-| Gate G02 Multiplayer/safety/live ops | Social play only if re-authorised. | Not P0; single player only. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Gate G03 Purchases/entitlements | Commerce only if re-authorised. | Not P0; no paid content. | Identity, legal, operations. |\n-| Deferred D01 Events, UGC, advanced analytics/monetisation | Extend later. | Explicitly deferred. | Content pipeline, connected policy. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness and target parity\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n+| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n+| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n+| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n \n-P0 completes J1\u2013J4 without G01\u2013G03 or D01: it is a complete local campaign, not a demo waiting on account, multiplayer, payment, or live-service work. Those interfaces cannot alter P0 rules, save ownership, or access to the ending.\n+## Cross-target contract\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web | PWA | Intentional difference |\n+| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign, rules, content, saves, settings/recovery | Full shared core. | Full shared core. | None. |\n-| Input/responsive UI | Keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad where supported; phone/tablet/desktop. | Same browser-supported model/layouts. | Capability may limit gamepad; alternate input remains. |\n-| Offline | Play after required assets cache; local campaign needs no connection. | Same. | PWA adds install/app-shell cache state. |\n-| Update safety | Saves separate from assets. | Same. | PWA stages/activates cache visibly without save mutation. |\n-| Account/commerce/connected services | Not P0. | Not P0. | None until approval. |\n+| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n+| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n+| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n+| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n+| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n+| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n+\n+## First-release completeness proof\n+\n+J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n \n-## Decisions carried forward\n+## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n \n-Implementation must not invent: engine/framework, repository/CI, browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, performance budget, update activation; party/content/balance counts; language/audio scope and exact accessibility settings; crash-reporting provider/consent/retention; or whether account sync, multiplayer, live operations, or commerce ship. Before any becomes a dependency, record its decision owner and its degraded, recovery, and deletion behavior.\n+Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n\ntokens used\n37,280\n", "adapter_log": "", "test_log": "Whitespace check passed after removing the trailing blank line at EOF.", "browser_log": "Not applicable: this task changes domain documentation only.", "screenshots": []}, "acceptance_results": [{"criterion": "Every feature maps to a user outcome and journey.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "The prioritised feature catalogue maps every listed feature to outcomes and J1\u2013J5 journeys."}, {"criterion": "Normal, empty, invalid, permission-denied, offline, conflict, recovery, and deletion paths are specified where relevant.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "Core journeys and each applicable domain chapter explicitly describe normal and relevant exceptional paths, including storage denial, offline operation, future remote-conflict boundary, recovery, and deletion."}, {"criterion": "The first release provides one complete useful journey without deferred dependencies.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "The First-release completeness proof establishes J1 from anonymous launch through first battle and confirmed local save, with no account, multiplayer, commerce, or live-service dependency."}, {"criterion": "Cross-target capability parity and intentional target-specific differences are documented.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "The Cross-target contract documents shared campaign/input/save behavior and intentional PWA install/cache/update differences."}, {"criterion": "Each domain chapter defines its entities, rules, lifecycle, authoring inputs, dependencies, player/user outcomes, edge cases, and first-release boundary.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "Twelve domain chapters cover shell, gameplay, content, platform, quality, and operations; each includes entities/lifecycle, rules/inputs/dependencies/outcome, edges, and release boundary."}, {"criterion": "The relationship map identifies cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries. For example, an RPG quest links narrative and save state; an IDE edit links language analysis, workspace indexing, and version control; an OS permission links identity, process, storage, and user settings.", "status": "pass", "evidence": "The system relationship map and six cross-domain invariants explicitly link narrative, quests, combat rewards, inventory, content packs, persistence, recovery, and PWA updates."}], "documentation": {"reviewed_files": ["projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/00-domain-discovery.md", "projects/README.md"], "updated_files": ["projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md"], "summary": "Added the canonical F002 feature-model specification. No README change was needed because this documents a project-specific domain model and does not alter repository usage."}, "known_limitations": ["Engine/framework, browser/device/performance thresholds, content quantities, localisation scope, accessibility conformance target, remote diagnostics policy, connected services, commerce, and legal/store policy remain approval decisions recorded in the specification."], "browser_coverage": "Not applicable: domain documentation only; no browser-facing implementation or Playwright specification changed.", "recommended_next_step": "complete"}


